WEBVTT

intro-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.410
Fourth quarter, rivalry game, and the stadium is shaking. Your roommate shoots for the student paper from the sideline, and by the final whistle, the camera will hold three thousand photos. Fans want them tonight. The editor wants the best ones online while the game is still on.

intro-2
00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:36.850
Every photo needs the same steps. Remove the noise from shooting at night. Brighten it. Crop it to the action. Blur the faces of fans in the stands, for privacy. Stamp the paper's watermark. Then publish it to the website. You're the one who keeps the photo desk's code running.

monolith-1
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:59.420
Here's the photo desk's script. Step one denoises all three thousand photos and saves them in a folder. Step two reads that folder, brightens every photo, and saves another. And so on, until the last step uploads everything. This design is called batch sequential. It all lives in one file, sharing a dozen global settings.

monolith-2
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:20.930
The camera finishes at ten p.m., and each step takes the script twenty minutes for the whole batch, so no photo goes online until every step has finished every photo: at midnight. The second-quarter catch waits behind three thousand others, five folders fill with in-between files, and fans post blurry phone shots instead.

monolith-3
00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:40.160
The quick fix is one loop that sends each photo through every step before starting the next. Photos now appear early. But it's still one file with shared settings: no step can be reused, reordered, or given its own processor core. Pipes and filters, this lesson's style, offers all four.

monolith-4
00:01:40.160 --> 00:02:02.220
And change hurts too. The editor wants a new step that straightens tilted shots, right after the crop. The robotics club wants your denoiser for its robot's camera. Both mean surgery on one giant file, where every step reads the same global settings. You need steps that work alone and pass each photo on.

filters-1
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:22.290
The pipes-and-filters style rebuilds the job as a chain of small programs called filters. In a component-and-connector view, which shows the system at run time, each filter is a component. It reads data through an input port, applies exactly one transformation to each item, and writes the result through an output port.

filters-2
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:42.540
Filters are joined by pipes, the connectors of this view. A pipe carries data from one filter's output port to the next filter's input port: in one direction, in order, and unchanged. Its small buffer decouples neighboring filters, so a fast producer and a slower consumer need not proceed in lockstep.

filters-3
00:02:42.540 --> 00:03:00.620
The chain begins at a source, which feeds in photos as the camera uploads them, and ends at a sink, the website gallery. Source and sink are the system's boundary with the outside world. A chain without branches, like this one, is called a pipeline.

alone-1
00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:23.230
These parts obey four rules, and each rule protects a property. Rule one, independence: filters share no state; they interact only through pipes. The global settings therefore become parameters of each filter. Because a filter depends on nothing outside its ports, it can be understood, tested, and reused in isolation.

alone-2
00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:44.920
Rule two, agnosticism: a filter must not know which filters come before or after it. Blur faces knows only its contract: a photo arrives at its input, and a photo leaves at its output. Since no filter names its neighbors, filters can be inserted, removed, or reordered without editing any of them.

alone-3
00:03:44.920 --> 00:04:07.420
Agnosticism has one precondition: neighboring filters must agree on the type of data that flows between them. Here, every pipe carries an image in one common file format. Because each filter accepts and produces that same type, any filter can follow any other, and the composition remains well formed.

stream-1
00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:27.750
Rule three, incremental processing: a filter transforms each item as it arrives and passes it on immediately, rather than waiting for the complete batch. As a result, the filters can all work at the same time: while Watermark stamps photo one, Blur faces can process photo two, and Crop photo three.

stream-2
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:43.490
Replay game night with the pipeline. Each filter takes about half a second per photo, and the camera uploads photos as they're shot. Predict: when does the second-quarter catch reach the website now?

stream-3
00:04:43.490 --> 00:05:02.640
Within seconds. The photo passes through five filters at about half a second each, and reaches the gallery while the crowd is still cheering. This is the purpose of incremental processing: it lowers the delay until the first result and keeps all five filters busy, so the pipeline keeps pace with the camera.

stream-4
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:28.100
Rule four, acyclic flow: data moves one way, from source to sink. Streams may split at a tee and merge at a join, but never loop back upstream. Together with independence and agnosticism, this promotes understandability: the pipeline's behavior is the composition of its filters, watermark of blur of crop, and so on.

order-1
00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:49.720
Every pipe carries the same format, so any order of filters connects. But does order change the result? Predict: Watermark stamps the paper's logo in a corner, and Crop trims the edges. Does the photo come out the same if Crop runs after Watermark instead of before?

order-2
00:05:49.720 --> 00:06:11.410
Not necessarily. Placing Crop after Watermark can cut the logo off the edge. Although the filters are independent, their order still determines the result, because function composition is not commutative: adding one and then doubling differs from doubling and then adding one. The same filters in a different order form a different pipeline.

order-3
00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:35.070
The rules therefore separate two responsibilities. A filter developer implements one filter against its input and output types, without knowledge of the surrounding pipeline. The pipeline builder selects the filters and their order. To straighten tilted shots, the builder inserts a new filter after Crop, and no existing filter is modified.

buys-1
00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:55.560
Now the consequences, each of which follows from a rule. First, reusability. The robotics club inserts your denoise filter into its own pipeline, and it works unchanged. Independence guarantees that it shares no state with your other filters, and agnosticism guarantees that it never referred to its neighbors.

buys-2
00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:18.610
Second, modifiability and extensibility. Inserting Straighten required one new filter and one new pipe, and replacing Brighten with an auto-exposure filter of the same type changed nothing else, because no filter names its neighbors. Testability follows: since a filter shares no state, a test can feed it a known photo and check its output.

buys-3
00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:40.490
Third, performance, measured as throughput, which follows from incremental processing: filters can work on different photos at the same time. In an allocation view that gives each filter its own processor core, the pipeline completes up to five times as many photos per minute. The slowest filter sets the pace.

costs-1
00:07:40.490 --> 00:08:04.420
The style also inhibits some qualities. First, interactive usability. The editor wants a brightness slider that updates every photo live, including photos already published. Each drag would send those photos from the gallery back into Brighten: a feedback loop. Acyclic flow forbids it, so interfaces that update as the user drags fit this style poorly.

costs-2
00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:25.910
Second, performance, measured as latency. So that any filter can follow any other, every pipe carries the same file format, and each filter must decode the image, perform its one task, and encode a new file. Five filters mean five decodes and five encodes: each photo takes longer than in one program.

costs-3
00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:50.630
Third, robustness: error handling. One corrupted photo crashes Blur faces. Which photos have left the pipeline, and which remain in the pipes? Under independence and agnosticism, no filter shares state or knows its neighbors, so no component knows the global state, and recovery is hard: a restart may publish some photos twice.

limits-1
00:08:50.630 --> 00:09:11.180
Some computations cannot be incremental. To keep only the best shot of each burst of ten, a filter must buffer all ten before it emits one, and a sort filter must read its entire input before producing any output. Downstream filters remain idle meanwhile, and the pipeline degrades toward a batch.

limits-2
00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:31.790
Other rules are bent deliberately. To caption players, a new filter reads the face filter's internal list of faces. This works, but it violates independence, and the two filters can no longer be reused separately or reordered freely. Each violated rule forfeits exactly the benefit that the rule provided.

limits-3
00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:52.850
In summary, pipes and filters suits workloads that form a stream of items processed by steps that need no shared state: photos, audio, logs, sensor data, or lines of text. It suits interactive applications poorly, as well as computations whose steps must share large amounts of data.

in_practice-1
00:09:52.850 --> 00:10:13.640
Pipes and filters appears beyond the photo desk. In a Unix shell, agnostic one-job tools are joined by pipes to count the most common words in a text. A graphics card renders every game frame as a pipeline: vertices are shaded, triangles rasterized into pixels, and pixels colored, many in parallel.

in_practice-2
00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:36.290
Elasticsearch passes every document's text through an analyzer: character filters, a tokenizer, and token filters that can lowercase and stem words. By default the same chain processes each full-text query, so a search for running shoes can find a running shoe. Fraud detectors on Apache Flink judge each card payment as it streams in.

in_practice-3
00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:59.660
Software radios turn antenna samples into music through a channel filter, a demodulator, and an audio filter, and GNU Radio has long enforced acyclic flow: its flowgraphs may contain no loops. Genomics labs run Nextflow pipelines for RNA sequencing, in which steps share no state, so each step can be tested and reused alone.

in_practice-4
00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:21.600
These systems share the workload the four rules assume: a stream of items transformed by independent steps. Where a step cannot obey them, the system bends a rule and pays for it: sort and the quality-control report wait for all their input, and the graphics card's last stage reads and writes shared buffers.

summary-1
00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:43.690
Let's recap. The photo desk's script ran each step on all three thousand photos, saved folders of in-between files, and published nothing until midnight. Pipes and filters decomposes the job into filters, components that each perform one transformation, joined by pipes, connectors that carry a stream from a source to a sink.

summary-2
00:11:43.690 --> 00:12:08.140
Four rules give the style its power. Independence and agnosticism, no shared state and no named neighbors, promote reusability and testability, modifiability and extensibility. Joined by acyclic flow, they promote understandability: the pipeline's behavior is the composition of its filters. Incremental processing promotes performance, measured as throughput.

summary-3
00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:35.380
The style inhibits three qualities. Interactive usability: acyclic flow forbids feedback upstream. Performance, measured as latency: every filter decodes and re-encodes the agreed format. Robustness: no component knows the global state to recover from a failure. Next time you write one giant loop over a list, ask: could each step be its own filter?
